{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The goal is to learn quickly whether an idea is worth pursuing, and the code will be thrown away, so reading it closely costs time and gains little. Keep real credentials, production data, and anything security-sensitive out of these experiments, and do not let the code quietly become the MVP."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo has to work reliably in front of an audience and has to represent the product honestly, so check the paths you will show and skim for hard-coded or faked behavior that could mislead stakeholders. Full line-by-line review is still more than a short-lived artifact needs."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, so read every change to authentication, authorization, payments, data storage, and user input line by line, and use tests and selective review for everything else. This is also when maintainability starts to matter, because the MVP codebase usually becomes the production codebase."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "A team has to own, debug, and extend this code for years, so every change needs a reviewer who understands it well enough to answer for it, whoever wrote it. AI can write most of the code, but it should pass the same code review, tests, and CI as human-written code."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical systems need traceability, accountability, and verification, so every line needs a qualified human reviewer plus whatever extra assurance your domain requires, such as independent review, formal verification, or audit trails. For the most critical components (safety interlocks, cryptography, core financial logic), consider level 1 or level 0, and check whether your regulatory framework restricts or requires disclosure of AI-generated code."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Review less closely when code is short-lived and mistakes are cheap, and more closely when code lasts longer, more people depend on it, and failures cost more. Moving from prototype to critical, the default goes from full vibe coding (4) to selective review backed by tests (3) to line-by-line review of every change (2). Two rules apply at every stage: security-sensitive code (auth, payments, secrets, user data handling) should always be read line by line once real users or data are involved, and code should never move into a later stage without being reviewed to that stage's standard. The most common failure is a vibe-coded prototype that quietly becomes production."
}

